system "ALA-LC:vai-Vaii:Latn:2010" {

metadata {
  authority_id alalc
  id 2010
  language iso-639-2:vai
  source_script Vaii
  destination_script Latn
  name "Vai Romanization Table (2010)"
  url https://www.loc.gov/catdir/cpso/romanization/vai.pdf
  creation_date 2010
  adoption_date
  description {    ALA-LC Vai Romanization Table, 2010 version. One-to-one
    romanization of the Vai syllabary as scoped by the table: 239
    syllable characters across the e, i, a, o, u, ɔ and ɛ vowel
    series (including the nasalized syllables and the ŋã, kpã, gbɔ̃,
    kpɛ̃, gɛ̃, ŋgɛ̃, ŋɔ̃, ŋɛ̃ forms) plus the syllabic nasal ꘋ (ŋ).
    Nasalized Latin vowels are normalized to the precomposed tilded
    letters (ẽ ĩ ã õ ũ) where they exist and to the combining tilde
    for ɔ̃ and ɛ̃. The extended Latin letters ɓ ɗ ɛ ŋ ɲ ɔ follow the
    table's Note 1 (Unicode standard usage approved by NDMSO, 2007).
  }

  notes {
    note "Note 1 (MARC-8): within a strictly MARC-8 environment the
    double underscore may be applied to a base character following
    LCRI 1.0E for ɓ ɗ ɛ ŋ ɲ ɔ (b̳ d̳ e̳ n̳ n̳ o̳); the ambiguity of n̳
    illustrates the advantage of extended Latin, long-standing in the
    Vai transcription tradition."
    note "Note 2 of the table excludes four categories of rarely used
    Vai characters: archaic logograms, Momolu Massaquoi's innovations
    (c. 1911), digits, and punctuation. In addition, the printed
    syllable grid does not include the twenty-eight syllable
    characters of the th, dh, sh and zh series of the Unicode Vai
    block (U+A50F U+A511 U+A517 U+A519 and the other twenty-four
    members); this omission is the source table's, and is recorded
    here so it is not mistaken for a transcription gap."
    note "The syllabic lengthener ꘌ is described in the table as
    repeating the preceding vowel; it is an instruction rather than a
    romanization value, so it carries no character rule."
    note "The syllables printed che chi cha cho chu chɔ chɛ correspond to
    the ch romanization of the table; the table uses ch rather than c
    before i and e."
  }
}

tests {
  "ꔀ" -> "e"
  "ꔁ" -> "ẽ"
  "ꕉ" -> "a"
  "ꕱ" -> "o"
  "ꖕ" -> "u"
  "ꖺ" -> "ɔ"
  "ꗡ" -> "ɛ"
  "ꖻ" -> "ɔ̃"
  "ꗢ" -> "ɛ̃"
  "ꘋ" -> "ŋ"
  "ꔀꔤꕉꕱꖕꗡ" -> "eiaouɛ"
}

